1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:05,860
Some SAT questions hand you a list of
bulleted notes and ask which sentence uses

2
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:06,420
them best.

3
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:09,020
They look like reading questions.

4
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:11,889
They're really about one thing: the goal.

5
00:00:12,301 --> 00:00:13,991
Here's a common mistake.

6
00:00:14,101 --> 00:00:19,401
Students read every note carefully, then
pick the choice that sounds the most

7
00:00:19,421 --> 00:00:21,231
complete or accurate.

8
00:00:21,261 --> 00:00:25,280
But on these questions, almost
every choice is accurate.

9
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:28,071
Only one does what the question asks.

10
00:00:28,522 --> 00:00:32,142
So in this video: the one habit
that fixes these questions,

11
00:00:32,162 --> 00:00:37,102
which is reading the goal first, and
six SAT-style questions covering common

12
00:00:37,122 --> 00:00:38,122
goals.

13
00:00:38,407 --> 00:00:41,536
These questions typically
open with the same line.

14
00:00:41,567 --> 00:00:45,817
While researching a topic, a student
has taken the following notes.

15
00:00:46,007 --> 00:00:48,507
Then comes a short list of bullet points,

16
00:00:48,527 --> 00:00:50,177
each one a fact.

17
00:00:50,642 --> 00:00:52,941
After the notes comes the question.

18
00:00:52,961 --> 00:00:55,932
The student wants to, and then a goal.

19
00:00:56,002 --> 00:00:58,172
Emphasize a similarity.

20
00:00:58,282 --> 00:01:00,982
Introduce a person to a new audience.

21
00:01:01,002 --> 00:01:03,632
Present a study and its findings.

22
00:01:03,652 --> 00:01:08,592
Then the same ending every time: which
choice most effectively uses relevant

23
00:01:08,612 --> 00:01:12,381
information from the notes
to accomplish this goal?

24
00:01:12,773 --> 00:01:14,353
Notice what's being tested.

25
00:01:14,373 --> 00:01:17,193
The notes are a pile of facts.

26
00:01:17,213 --> 00:01:21,033
The four choices are usually
built from those same facts,

27
00:01:21,053 --> 00:01:23,033
so they're usually all true.

28
00:01:23,053 --> 00:01:26,353
The SAT isn't asking
which sentence is true.

29
00:01:26,373 --> 00:01:28,983
It's asking which sentence does the job.

30
00:01:29,431 --> 00:01:30,521
Here's the method.

31
00:01:30,751 --> 00:01:35,361
Step one: skip down and read
the goal before the notes.

32
00:01:35,471 --> 00:01:38,371
Underline the key words, like similarity,

33
00:01:38,391 --> 00:01:42,041
difference, unfamiliar
audience, or findings.

34
00:01:42,572 --> 00:01:46,022
Step two: turn the goal into a checklist.

35
00:01:46,172 --> 00:01:50,791
Emphasize a similarity means the
answer has to mention both things,

36
00:01:50,811 --> 00:01:52,622
and show what they share.

37
00:01:52,731 --> 00:01:55,182
One thing alone isn't enough.

38
00:01:55,695 --> 00:01:59,675
Step three: test each choice
against that checklist.

39
00:01:59,695 --> 00:02:01,115
Not, is it true.

40
00:02:01,135 --> 00:02:02,675
Not, is it interesting.

41
00:02:02,695 --> 00:02:04,264
Does it do the goal?

42
00:02:04,575 --> 00:02:09,795
Step four: look back at the notes
only if you need to check a detail.

43
00:02:09,815 --> 00:02:11,865
Most of the time, you won't.

44
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:13,330
Let's try it.

45
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:15,010
Goal first.

46
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:21,010
The student wants to emphasize a
similarity between bats and dolphins.

47
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:23,530
Key word: similarity.

48
00:02:23,879 --> 00:02:28,410
So the answer has to mention both
animals and something they share.

49
00:02:28,915 --> 00:02:30,205
Now the notes.

50
00:02:30,275 --> 00:02:34,055
Bats are the only mammals
capable of true flight.

51
00:02:34,075 --> 00:02:38,255
Many bats hunt insects in
the dark using echolocation:

52
00:02:38,275 --> 00:02:41,054
they make sounds and
listen for the echoes.

53
00:02:41,074 --> 00:02:44,815
Dolphins are marine mammals
that hunt fish and squid.

54
00:02:44,835 --> 00:02:48,605
Dolphins also locate
prey using echolocation.

55
00:02:48,625 --> 00:02:53,355
Bats make high-pitched calls, while
dolphins produce rapid clicks.

56
00:02:53,820 --> 00:02:55,230
Pause and try it.

57
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,460
A says bats can truly fly, while
dolphins are marine mammals.

58
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:07,770
That's a difference.

59
00:03:07,880 --> 00:03:08,860
Out.

60
00:03:08,880 --> 00:03:11,059
B is only about dolphins.

61
00:03:11,079 --> 00:03:13,489
A similarity needs both animals.

62
00:03:13,520 --> 00:03:14,520
Out.

63
00:03:14,772 --> 00:03:19,952
D says bats make high-pitched calls,
whereas dolphins produce rapid clicks.

64
00:03:19,972 --> 00:03:21,952
Whereas is a contrast word.

65
00:03:21,972 --> 00:03:23,341
That's a difference too.

66
00:03:23,813 --> 00:03:29,553
C says both bats and dolphins
use echolocation to locate prey.

67
00:03:29,573 --> 00:03:31,993
Both animals, one shared trait.

68
00:03:32,013 --> 00:03:33,553
The answer is C.

69
00:03:33,573 --> 00:03:36,702
And notice: every choice was true.

70
00:03:37,013 --> 00:03:39,513
Accuracy didn't eliminate anything.

71
00:03:39,533 --> 00:03:40,583
The goal did.

72
00:03:41,076 --> 00:03:44,766
Before the next questions, here
are goals that show up often.

73
00:03:44,915 --> 00:03:47,646
Compare or contrast two things.

74
00:03:47,876 --> 00:03:50,046
Emphasize a similarity.

75
00:03:50,315 --> 00:03:55,126
Introduce someone or something to an
audience that's unfamiliar with it.

76
00:03:55,395 --> 00:03:58,966
Present a study, its
method, or its findings.

77
00:03:59,036 --> 00:04:02,686
Explain an advantage,
a cause, or a purpose.

78
00:04:03,135 --> 00:04:05,344
Each goal has its own checklist.

79
00:04:05,374 --> 00:04:08,475
Contrast needs both
things and a difference.

80
00:04:08,495 --> 00:04:13,674
An unfamiliar audience needs an
explanation of who or what something is.

81
00:04:13,694 --> 00:04:16,514
Findings means what the
researchers discovered,

82
00:04:16,534 --> 00:04:18,265
not just what they did.

83
00:04:18,785 --> 00:04:20,005
Question two.

84
00:04:20,025 --> 00:04:21,675
Goal first.

85
00:04:21,985 --> 00:04:26,875
The student wants to emphasize a difference
in where the two telescopes operate.

86
00:04:26,944 --> 00:04:30,995
Two key words here: difference, and where.

87
00:04:31,451 --> 00:04:32,541
The notes.

88
00:04:33,011 --> 00:04:36,791
The Hubble Space Telescope
launched in nineteen ninety.

89
00:04:36,811 --> 00:04:40,711
It orbits Earth a few hundred
miles above the surface.

90
00:04:40,731 --> 00:04:44,901
The James Webb Space Telescope
launched in twenty twenty-one.

91
00:04:44,930 --> 00:04:48,861
It orbits the Sun about one
million miles from Earth.

92
00:04:48,941 --> 00:04:53,121
Both telescopes were built through
international partnerships that include NASA

93
00:04:53,141 --> 00:04:55,471
and the European Space Agency.

94
00:04:55,962 --> 00:04:57,372
Pause and try it.

95
00:05:04,143 --> 00:05:05,523
A is a similarity.

96
00:05:05,543 --> 00:05:06,362
Out.

97
00:05:06,382 --> 00:05:08,123
C only describes Webb.

98
00:05:08,143 --> 00:05:10,483
You can't show a difference
with one telescope.

99
00:05:10,503 --> 00:05:11,513
Out.

100
00:05:11,953 --> 00:05:12,573
B is the trap.

101
00:05:12,593 --> 00:05:18,133
It is a difference: Hubble launched
more than thirty years before Webb.

102
00:05:18,153 --> 00:05:21,332
But the goal said a difference in where,

103
00:05:21,352 --> 00:05:22,602
not when.

104
00:05:22,913 --> 00:05:25,643
Right type of answer, wrong detail.

105
00:05:26,067 --> 00:05:30,247
D puts Hubble orbiting Earth
next to Webb orbiting the Sun,

106
00:05:30,267 --> 00:05:32,117
a million miles away.

107
00:05:32,347 --> 00:05:34,927
A difference, and it's about location.

108
00:05:34,947 --> 00:05:36,677
The answer is D.

109
00:05:37,152 --> 00:05:38,332
Question three.

110
00:05:38,352 --> 00:05:44,522
The student wants to introduce Zaha
Hadid to an audience unfamiliar with her.

111
00:05:44,752 --> 00:05:48,172
That phrase, unfamiliar
audience, is a signal.

112
00:05:48,192 --> 00:05:53,242
The reader has never heard of her, so
the sentence has to tell them who she is.

113
00:05:53,641 --> 00:05:54,641
The notes.

114
00:05:55,121 --> 00:05:59,621
Zaha Hadid, who lived from
nineteen fifty to twenty sixteen,

115
00:05:59,641 --> 00:06:02,260
was an Iraqi-born British architect.

116
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:04,661
She was known for buildings with flowing,

117
00:06:04,681 --> 00:06:06,020
curved forms.

118
00:06:06,040 --> 00:06:10,061
In two thousand four, she became
the first woman to win the Pritzker

119
00:06:10,081 --> 00:06:13,731
Architecture Prize, one of
architecture's highest honors.

120
00:06:13,751 --> 00:06:18,521
She designed the London Aquatics Centre
for the twenty twelve Olympic Games.

121
00:06:19,041 --> 00:06:20,451
Pause and try it.

122
00:06:27,221 --> 00:06:30,560
A says, Hadid designed the
London Aquatics Centre.

123
00:06:30,580 --> 00:06:33,281
If you've never heard of her, you'd ask:

124
00:06:33,301 --> 00:06:33,721
who's Hadid?

125
00:06:33,741 --> 00:06:34,481
D has the same problem.

126
00:06:34,501 --> 00:06:38,231
It talks about Hadid's buildings
but never says who she is.

127
00:06:38,981 --> 00:06:42,881
B is about the prize going to
a woman for the first time,

128
00:06:42,901 --> 00:06:44,401
but it never names her.

129
00:06:44,421 --> 00:06:47,310
You can't introduce someone
without naming them.

130
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:52,580
C: Zaha Hadid, an Iraqi-born
British architect known for flowing,

131
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:57,340
curved buildings, was the first woman
to win the Pritzker Architecture Prize.

132
00:06:57,360 --> 00:07:01,009
Full name, who she is,
and why she matters.

133
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:02,730
The answer is C.

134
00:07:03,157 --> 00:07:04,247
Quick tip.

135
00:07:04,677 --> 00:07:09,297
For an unfamiliar audience, look for
a description right next to the name,

136
00:07:09,317 --> 00:07:11,376
usually set off by commas.

137
00:07:11,396 --> 00:07:14,616
If the goal said the
audience already knew her,

138
00:07:14,636 --> 00:07:16,687
that description wouldn't be needed.

139
00:07:17,157 --> 00:07:18,367
Question four.

140
00:07:18,437 --> 00:07:22,327
The student wants to present
the study and its findings.

141
00:07:22,477 --> 00:07:23,567
Two parts.

142
00:07:23,797 --> 00:07:26,767
The study means who did what.

143
00:07:27,117 --> 00:07:29,687
The findings mean what they discovered.

144
00:07:30,166 --> 00:07:31,166
The notes.

145
00:07:32,006 --> 00:07:36,706
Arctic terns are seabirds that breed
in the far north and spend the northern

146
00:07:36,726 --> 00:07:39,096
winter in Antarctic waters.

147
00:07:39,165 --> 00:07:42,225
A research team attached
tiny tracking devices,

148
00:07:42,245 --> 00:07:44,785
each weighing about one point four grams,

149
00:07:44,805 --> 00:07:48,556
to Arctic terns nesting
in Greenland and Iceland.

150
00:07:48,576 --> 00:07:53,836
The researchers used the devices to
follow the birds' yearly migration.

151
00:07:53,856 --> 00:07:58,436
They found that the terns traveled an
average of about seventy thousand nine

152
00:07:58,456 --> 00:08:00,786
hundred kilometers in a year.

153
00:08:01,239 --> 00:08:02,649
Pause and try it.

154
00:08:09,419 --> 00:08:11,678
B is background about the bird.

155
00:08:11,698 --> 00:08:13,839
No study, no findings.

156
00:08:13,859 --> 00:08:17,239
C describes what the researchers did,

157
00:08:17,259 --> 00:08:20,359
the tracking devices,
but not what they found.

158
00:08:20,379 --> 00:08:21,828
That's half the goal.

159
00:08:22,303 --> 00:08:24,843
D gives the finding, but drops the study.

160
00:08:24,863 --> 00:08:27,803
It reads like a general fact about terns,

161
00:08:27,823 --> 00:08:29,473
not a research result.

162
00:08:29,919 --> 00:08:31,609
A has both.

163
00:08:31,639 --> 00:08:36,259
Researchers used tracking devices
on terns from Greenland and Iceland,

164
00:08:36,279 --> 00:08:40,939
and found the birds traveled about
seventy thousand nine hundred kilometers a

165
00:08:40,959 --> 00:08:41,699
year.

166
00:08:41,719 --> 00:08:43,328
The answer is A.

167
00:08:43,753 --> 00:08:46,092
Let's name the traps you've seen so far.

168
00:08:46,112 --> 00:08:49,133
Trap one: true, but off goal.

169
00:08:49,153 --> 00:08:51,053
Every fact checks out.

170
00:08:51,073 --> 00:08:52,843
It just does a different job.

171
00:08:53,319 --> 00:08:56,379
Trap two: right type, wrong detail.

172
00:08:56,399 --> 00:08:59,299
A difference, but in
when instead of where.

173
00:08:59,319 --> 00:09:01,808
Trap three: half the goal.

174
00:09:01,999 --> 00:09:05,728
The study without the findings,
or the findings without the study.

175
00:09:06,167 --> 00:09:08,577
Trap four: one side only.

176
00:09:08,607 --> 00:09:12,337
If the goal involves two things,
the answer has to mention both.

177
00:09:12,867 --> 00:09:14,277
Question five.

178
00:09:14,387 --> 00:09:20,437
The student wants to explain an advantage
of LED bulbs over incandescent bulbs.

179
00:09:20,507 --> 00:09:26,196
Checklist: it has to be about LEDs, and
it has to say what makes them better.

180
00:09:26,624 --> 00:09:27,644
The notes.

181
00:09:27,664 --> 00:09:33,484
Incandescent bulbs make light by heating
a thin wire filament until it glows.

182
00:09:33,504 --> 00:09:39,124
About ninety percent of the energy an
incandescent bulb uses is given off as

183
00:09:39,144 --> 00:09:40,044
heat.

184
00:09:40,064 --> 00:09:45,124
LED bulbs make light using tiny electronic
components called light-emitting

185
00:09:45,144 --> 00:09:45,974
diodes.

186
00:09:45,994 --> 00:09:47,054
According to the U.S.

187
00:09:47,074 --> 00:09:52,694
Department of Energy, residential LED
bulbs use at least seventy-five percent

188
00:09:52,714 --> 00:09:59,084
less energy than incandescent bulbs and
can last up to twenty-five times longer.

189
00:09:59,566 --> 00:10:00,976
Pause and try it.

190
00:10:06,746 --> 00:10:09,806
A and B explain how each bulb works.

191
00:10:09,826 --> 00:10:13,686
True, but how something
works isn't an advantage.

192
00:10:13,706 --> 00:10:15,326
C is closer.

193
00:10:15,346 --> 00:10:18,116
It's a weakness of incandescent bulbs.

194
00:10:18,426 --> 00:10:23,876
But it never mentions LEDs, so it
can't explain an advantage of LEDs.

195
00:10:24,366 --> 00:10:29,746
D says LED bulbs use at least seventy-five
percent less energy and can last up

196
00:10:29,766 --> 00:10:31,506
to twenty-five times longer.

197
00:10:31,526 --> 00:10:34,626
That's an advantage,
stated directly about LEDs.

198
00:10:34,646 --> 00:10:36,656
The answer is D.

199
00:10:37,094 --> 00:10:39,384
Last one, and it's harder.

200
00:10:39,453 --> 00:10:44,954
The student wants to emphasize that the
spacecraft that launched first was not

201
00:10:44,974 --> 00:10:47,784
the first to reach interstellar space.

202
00:10:48,223 --> 00:10:49,223
The notes.

203
00:10:49,623 --> 00:10:54,363
In nineteen seventy-seven,
NASA launched two spacecraft,

204
00:10:54,383 --> 00:10:57,083
Voyager one and Voyager two.

205
00:10:57,103 --> 00:11:00,193
Voyager two launched on August twentieth.

206
00:11:00,462 --> 00:11:03,762
Voyager one launched sixteen days later,

207
00:11:03,782 --> 00:11:05,593
on September fifth.

208
00:11:05,743 --> 00:11:09,633
Voyager one reached interstellar
space in twenty twelve.

209
00:11:09,673 --> 00:11:13,763
Voyager two reached interstellar
space in twenty eighteen.

210
00:11:13,873 --> 00:11:18,683
Voyager two is the only spacecraft
to have visited Uranus and Neptune.

211
00:11:19,118 --> 00:11:20,378
Pause and try it.

212
00:11:20,398 --> 00:11:22,087
Take a little longer on this one.

213
00:11:30,897 --> 00:11:32,237
Break the goal down.

214
00:11:32,257 --> 00:11:36,277
To show that the first to launch
wasn't the first to arrive,

215
00:11:36,297 --> 00:11:40,317
the sentence needs two things:
which one launched first,

216
00:11:40,337 --> 00:11:42,386
and which one arrived first.

217
00:11:42,849 --> 00:11:44,869
D is about Uranus and Neptune.

218
00:11:44,889 --> 00:11:48,429
Interesting, but off goal.

219
00:11:48,449 --> 00:11:51,109
C gives the launch order only.

220
00:11:51,129 --> 00:11:56,629
A gives the arrival order only: Voyager
one got there six years before Voyager

221
00:11:56,649 --> 00:11:57,509
two.

222
00:11:57,529 --> 00:12:00,939
A is the tempting one, because
it sounds like the main point.

223
00:12:01,332 --> 00:12:04,671
But A never says Voyager
two launched first,

224
00:12:04,691 --> 00:12:06,472
so there's nothing surprising about it.

225
00:12:06,492 --> 00:12:09,062
Only B has both halves.

226
00:12:09,212 --> 00:12:13,512
Although Voyager two launched
sixteen days before Voyager one,

227
00:12:13,532 --> 00:12:17,872
it reached interstellar space
six years after its twin.

228
00:12:17,892 --> 00:12:19,662
The answer is B.

229
00:12:20,113 --> 00:12:22,613
Here's the whole method on one card.

230
00:12:22,633 --> 00:12:26,573
One: read the goal first
and underline the key words.

231
00:12:26,593 --> 00:12:28,652
Two: turn it into a checklist.

232
00:12:28,672 --> 00:12:30,533
What has to be in the answer?

233
00:12:30,553 --> 00:12:31,252
Both things?

234
00:12:31,272 --> 00:12:32,172
A description?

235
00:12:32,192 --> 00:12:33,323
The findings?

236
00:12:33,863 --> 00:12:37,003
Three: test each choice
against the checklist,

237
00:12:37,023 --> 00:12:38,833
not against the notes.

238
00:12:39,223 --> 00:12:42,833
Four: check the notes
only to settle a detail.

239
00:12:43,063 --> 00:12:46,752
And if the goal has two parts,
make sure the answer has both.

240
00:12:47,193 --> 00:12:48,293
One pacing note.

241
00:12:48,313 --> 00:12:52,453
You get about seventy-one seconds
per question in Reading and Writing,

242
00:12:52,473 --> 00:12:53,643
on average.

243
00:12:53,833 --> 00:12:56,203
Because you aren't studying every note,

244
00:12:56,473 --> 00:12:59,453
a notes question can
often take less than that,

245
00:12:59,473 --> 00:13:02,403
which buys time for harder questions.

246
00:13:02,861 --> 00:13:03,911
Recap.

247
00:13:04,301 --> 00:13:07,801
On notes questions, the
choices are usually all true,

248
00:13:07,821 --> 00:13:09,471
so the goal decides.

249
00:13:09,501 --> 00:13:13,000
Read it first, make a checklist, and
cross out anything that does a different

250
00:13:14,020 --> 00:13:15,791
job, even if it's accurate.

251
00:13:16,244 --> 00:13:20,344
For more practice, the College Board's
free Question Bank lets you filter for

252
00:13:20,364 --> 00:13:24,134
rhetorical synthesis
under Expression of Ideas.

253
00:13:24,623 --> 00:13:28,083
All the questions in this video
were written for this video,

254
00:13:28,103 --> 00:13:30,473
in the style of the real test.

255
00:13:30,543 --> 00:13:32,753
